(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-pericardial-disease-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Pericardial Disease Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with metastatic lung cancer has a symptomatic malignant pericardial effusion that has already reaccumulated once after drainage. Which consult is recommended to improve her outcomes and quality of life?","choices":["Endocrinology","Palliative care","Physical therapy","Rheumatology"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A symptomatic malignant pericardial effusion carries a limited life expectancy, a median of 2 to 4 months, and often recurs within that time, as hers already has. A palliative care consult is recommended to improve clinical outcomes and quality of life. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man was treated for acute pericarditis 3 months ago and has been symptom-free for 6 weeks. His sharp, positional chest pain has now returned with a friction rub (a scratchy, squeaking sound). C-reactive protein is mildly raised. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Anakinra plus prednisone at once","Ibuprofen with colchicine for 6 months or more","Ibuprofen with colchicine, stopped at 2 weeks","Prednisone alone for 6 months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His pain returned after 6 weeks symptom-free, a first recurrence. That is treated with nonsteroidal drugs until chest pain resolves and inflammatory markers normalize, plus colchicine for 6 months or more. An interleukin-1 blocker is not needed yet, since his C-reactive protein is only mildly raised and he is not colchicine-resistant. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has had 10 days of gradually worsening pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) chest pain without a clear onset. His temperature is 38.7 degrees Celsius (101.7 degrees Fahrenheit), and the echocardiogram shows a moderate pericardial effusion without collapse of any chamber. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassurance and repeat echo in 3 months","Immediate pericardiectomy","Hospital admission for workup","Outpatient ibuprofen and colchicine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A temperature of 38.7 degrees Celsius (above 38), a subacute course over 10 days without an acute onset of chest pain, and a moderate effusion are each associated with higher risk and a potential need for hospitalization. He should be admitted for evaluation rather than treated as routine outpatient pericarditis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has needed prednisone for several months for pericarditis that did not respond to first-line therapy. Which complication of her treatment should be specifically anticipated?","choices":["Hypoglycemia","Bradycardia","Hearing loss","Osteoporosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Corticosteroids increase the risk of osteoporosis, and in a 76-year-old on months of prednisone that risk should be specifically anticipated. Older adults also call for caution with anti-inflammatory drugs on the Beers list, with colchicine considered for dose reduction. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man is diagnosed with acute idiopathic pericarditis after a viral illness. He is afebrile, his troponin is normal, the echocardiogram shows no effusion, and he has no contraindication to any medication. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Ibuprofen plus colchicine","Prednisone plus colchicine","Anakinra plus colchicine","Vancomycin plus ceftriaxone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. He has acute idiopathic pericarditis after a viral illness with no high-risk features (afebrile, normal troponin, no effusion) and no contraindications. A nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug such as ibuprofen plus colchicine is the initial recommended therapy for most such patients, with gastrointestinal protection. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman recovering well from her first episode of acute viral pericarditis asks how likely it is to come back. Which estimate is most accurate?","choices":["Under 1%","About 15 to 30%","About 60%","Over 90%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent pericarditis affects about 15 to 30% of patients after an episode of acute pericarditis. Recurrence is therefore common enough to plan for, although most patients do not have another episode. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has a new moderate pericardial effusion with joint aches and fatigue. She has not traveled and has no recent viral illness. Which test is most reasonable to add to her workup?","choices":["Antinuclear antibody","Blood cultures","Serum lipase","Hemoglobin A1c"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The effusion workup includes a complete blood count, chemistry with renal function, thyroid-stimulating hormone and troponins, and possibly an antinuclear antibody, for example in a young woman with an effusion. At 27, with joint aches and fatigue and no travel or viral illness, she is that patient. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man is discharged after a large viral pericardial effusion was found without tamponade. He asks how the effusion will be monitored. Which follow-up plan is most appropriate?","choices":["Serial transthoracic echocardiograms","Monthly electrocardiograms alone","Serial chest radiographs alone","No follow-up once symptoms ease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. His effusion is large, and all large pericardial effusions should be followed with serial transthoracic echocardiograms, the gold-standard test that shows the effusion and any right ventricular diastolic collapse. Close clinical follow-up with the specialty matching the cause rounds out the plan. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman had a large effusion drained for tamponade. Afterward her jugular venous pressure remains elevated and shows the Kussmaul sign (a rise on inspiration), and she remains fluid overloaded. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Effusive-constrictive pericarditis","Recurrent acute pericarditis","Simple pericardial effusion","Restrictive cardiomyopathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An elevated jugular venous pressure with the Kussmaul sign and ongoing fluid overload after a tamponading effusion was drained show constriction persisting after drainage. Effusive-constrictive pericarditis is underlying constriction with a coexisting effusion, usually with tamponade, which is exactly this sequence. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman with right lower lobe pneumonia has sharp chest pain. A rubbing sound is heard over the lower chest that is present only while she breathes in and vanishes when she holds her breath. Which is the most likely source of the sound?","choices":["The pericardium","The mitral valve","The chest wall","The pleura"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her rub is heard only during inspiration and vanishes with breath-holding, the pattern of a pleural rub, which fits her right lower lobe pneumonia. A pericardial rub behaves differently: its intensity increases when the patient holds a breath, leans forward or the diaphragm is pressed firmly. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man who recently moved from a region where tuberculosis is common has effusive pericarditis. Pericardial fluid is positive on acid-fast stain. Which treatment may significantly reduce his progression to constriction?","choices":["Antituberculous therapy","Early pericardiectomy","Long-term loop diuretic","Indefinite aspirin alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. He comes from a region where tuberculosis is common and his pericardial fluid is positive on acid-fast stain, so tuberculosis is the underlying etiology. When tuberculosis is the cause, treating it may significantly reduce progression to constrictive pericarditis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 67-year-old former smoker with weight loss has a large pericardial effusion. Lung cancer is suspected, and pericardiocentesis is performed. Which study of the pericardial fluid best addresses this suspicion?","choices":["Gram stain","Fungal culture","Cell count","Cytology"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A former smoker with weight loss and a large effusion raises suspicion of lung cancer, a malignancy to evaluate for in the effusion workup. Of the studies sent on pericardial fluid (cell count, Gram stain, bacterial and fungal culture, acid-fast stain with mycobacterial culture, and cytology), cytology is the one aimed at malignant cells. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with advanced constrictive pericarditis and moderate biatrial enlargement reports new palpitations. His pulse is irregularly irregular. Which rhythm is he most prone to?","choices":["Complete heart block","Ventricular tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation","Atrial flutter with 2:1 block"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation is common in advanced constrictive pericarditis, and new palpitations with an irregularly irregular pulse in a man with advanced disease fit it. Other electrocardiogram findings in constriction are nonspecific ST- and T-wave changes, tachycardia and sometimes low voltage. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with tamponade from a newly found effusion and no known cancer is being evaluated. Which consideration about the cause is most important at her age?","choices":["Dressler syndrome is most likely","Her age makes the cause irrelevant","A higher suspicion for malignancy","Coxsackievirus is the likely cause"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In older adults, an effusion or tamponade raises the index of suspicion for malignancy. At 72, with a newly found effusion causing tamponade and no known cancer, the evaluation should actively look for an underlying malignancy. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man had a tuberculous pericardial effusion drained in the hospital and is starting antituberculous therapy. Which specialty should be part of his close follow-up?","choices":["Infectious disease","Nephrology","Rheumatology","Medical oncology"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Close follow-up after a pericardial effusion is matched to the underlying etiology, and his is tuberculosis on antituberculous therapy, an infection, so infectious disease belongs in his care. Long-term prognosis also depends on the underlying cause, which makes treating that cause central. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with acute pericarditis is starting ibuprofen and colchicine. He has a history of dyspepsia. Which addition to his regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["An oral anticoagulant","A prophylactic antibiotic","A loop diuretic","A proton pump inhibitor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ibuprofen and colchicine are the initial therapy for most acute pericarditis, and gastrointestinal protection is provided alongside anti-inflammatory treatment, for example a proton pump inhibitor such as omeprazole. His history of dyspepsia makes that protection especially relevant. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 26-year-old marathon runner is diagnosed with acute viral pericarditis and starts ibuprofen and colchicine. He asks when he can return to training. Which advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Run on alternate days during treatment","Avoid exercise for the rest of his life","Avoid strenuous activity for at least 1 month","Resume running once the chest pain stops"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Strenuous physical activity such as marathon training is avoided until symptoms resolve and biomarkers such as C-reactive protein normalize, for at least 1 month after diagnosis, with a maximal heart rate under 100 beats per minute. He can return once all of those conditions are met. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has had three episodes of pericarditis in 18 months, each treated in primary care with an anti-inflammatory and colchicine. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Cardiothoracic surgery","Cardiology","Infectious disease","Gastroenterology"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Three episodes in 18 months is recurrent pericarditis, and recurrences are a stated reason to refer to cardiology for further assessment. The other referral triggers are no response to conservative management and development of constrictive pericarditis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with constrictive pericarditis is scheduled for pericardiectomy next week. He takes a loop diuretic for leg edema. How should the diuretic be handled before surgery?","choices":["Add a second diuretic","Use it sparingly","Double the dose","Give it intravenously"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. He has constrictive pericarditis with pericardiectomy planned next week, and when surgery is planned, diuretics should be used sparingly in the preoperative period. Diuretics still relieve fluid overload in constriction, which is why the advice is sparing use rather than escalation. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with recurrent pericarditis over several years now has leg edema, ascites and an elevated jugular venous pressure. Constrictive pericarditis is suspected. Which echocardiogram finding would best support it?","choices":["Right ventricular diastolic collapse","Asymmetric septal hypertrophy","A dilated, weakly contracting ventricle","Septal bounce toward the left ventricle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Years of recurrent pericarditis, a listed antecedent of constriction, now with edema, ascites and an elevated jugular venous pressure fit constrictive pericarditis. A septal bounce toward the left ventricle is one of its echocardiographic features, along with increased pericardial thickness, pronounced respiratory variation in ventricular filling and moderate biatrial enlargement. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man is readmitted 3 weeks after coronary artery bypass surgery with fever, pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) chest pain eased by sitting forward, and a friction rub (a scratchy, squeaking sound). His sternal wound looks clean. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dressler syndrome","Sternal wound infection","Acute graft occlusion","Constrictive pericarditis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fever, pleuritic pain eased by sitting forward and a friction rub 3 weeks after coronary artery bypass surgery, with a clean sternal wound, point to post-cardiotomy pericarditis. Pericarditis after myocardial infarction or after cardiac surgery is Dressler syndrome, a listed cause of pericardial disease. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman is hypotensive with distended neck veins and a large effusion with right ventricular diastolic collapse. After pericardiocentesis removes 600 mL, her blood pressure rises from 80/50 to 118/74 and her dyspnea resolves. What does this response establish?","choices":["It confirms constrictive disease","It shows the echo was misread","It confirms the tamponade diagnosis","It rules out a malignant cause"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac tamponade is a clinical diagnosis: echocardiography identifies the effusion and its hemodynamic significance, but the diagnosis can only be confirmed by the hemodynamic and clinical response to drainage. Her rise from 80/50 to 118/74 with resolved dyspnea after removing 600 mL is that confirmation. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman has taken ibuprofen and colchicine for 8 days for presumed viral pericarditis. She still has a temperature of 38.5 degrees Celsius and pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) pain, and a new pericardial effusion has appeared on echocardiogram. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Stop colchicine and add a diuretic","Start anakinra as a first step","Admit and look for another cause","Continue the same drugs for a month"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. After 8 days of ibuprofen and colchicine she still has a temperature of 38.5 degrees Celsius and pleuritic pain, and a new effusion has appeared, which meets the definition of treatment failure. Failure to respond within 1 week suggests a cause other than idiopathic or viral pericarditis, so she needs inpatient evaluation for that cause. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has fatigue, exertional dyspnea, an elevated jugular venous pressure with the Kussmaul sign (a rise on inspiration), ascites and minimal ankle edema. His liver enzymes are mildly raised. Which condition is the key noncardiac mimic to consider?","choices":["Hyperthyroidism","Cirrhosis","Asthma","Pneumonia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cirrhosis is the noncardiac condition in the differential of constrictive pericarditis, alongside subacute tamponade and restrictive cardiomyopathy. His ascites with little ankle edema and mildly raised liver enzymes could mislead toward the liver; echocardiographic features such as increased pericardial thickness and septal bounce help confirm constriction. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man had an anterior myocardial infarction 3 weeks ago. He now has fever, pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) chest pain eased by sitting forward, and a friction rub (a scratchy, three-part rubbing sound) at the left sternal border. Which drug should be avoided in treating his pericarditis?","choices":["Aspirin","Colchicine","Pantoprazole","Ibuprofen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fever, pleuritic pain eased by sitting forward and a rub 3 weeks after an anterior infarction make this post-infarction pericarditis. Nonsteroidal drugs such as ibuprofen are not given for post-infarction pericarditis, and in patients on aspirin as an antiplatelet agent, ibuprofen and indomethacin are avoided. Aspirin plus colchicine is used. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man who grew up in a country where tuberculosis is common has fever, night sweats and a moderate pericardial effusion. Fluid is obtained by pericardiocentesis. Which fluid studies best address the suspected cause?","choices":["Gram stain and routine culture","Antinuclear antibody on the fluid","Acid-fast stain and mycobacterial culture","Cytology for malignant cells alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Growing up where tuberculosis is common, with fever, night sweats and a moderate effusion, raises suspicion for tuberculous pericarditis, an indication for diagnostic pericardiocentesis. The fluid studies aimed at it are the acid-fast bacilli stain and mycobacterial culture, sent alongside cell count, Gram stain, cultures and cytology. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"An 81-year-old man with 3 months of weight loss presents with dyspnea and a large pericardial effusion. He has had no recent viral illness, chest surgery or radiation. Which etiology should be most strongly suspected?","choices":["Dressler syndrome","Rheumatic fever","Malignancy","Coxsackievirus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In older adults there is a higher index of suspicion for malignancy, especially with an effusion or tamponade. At 81, with 3 months of weight loss, a large effusion and no viral illness, surgery or radiation to explain it, malignancy is the cause to pursue. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman treated for breast cancer 2 years ago has 3 weeks of fatigue and exertional dyspnea. Her blood pressure is 128/80 without hypotension. The chest radiograph shows an enlarged, water-bottle (globular) cardiac silhouette with clear lung fields. Which test is required to confirm a pericardial effusion?","choices":["Repeat chest radiograph","Twelve-lead electrocardiogram","Coronary angiography","Transthoracic echocardiogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her breast cancer history, 3 weeks of fatigue and dyspnea, and a globular silhouette with clear lungs suggest an effusion, but clinical evaluation, electrocardiogram and chest radiograph can only suggest it. An echocardiogram is required to confirm a pericardial effusion, and transthoracic echocardiography is part of the initial workup. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with renal failure has a large pericardial effusion and borderline blood pressure. His chest radiograph shows a markedly enlarged, water-bottle (globular) cardiac silhouette. What do his lung fields most likely show?","choices":["Lobar consolidation","Clear on both sides","Bat-wing pulmonary edema","Bilateral hilar adenopathy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A large pericardial effusion appears on chest radiograph as cardiomegaly with a globular, water-bottle silhouette, and clear lung fields may be present. At least 200 mL of fluid must accumulate before the silhouette enlarges, so his markedly enlarged heart reflects a large collection. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man comes to a primary care office with dyspnea, lightheadedness, distended neck veins and a blood pressure of 86/58. He was diagnosed with a pericardial effusion last month. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Schedule cardiology in two weeks","Send him to the emergency department","Order an outpatient echocardiogram","Start ibuprofen and colchicine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dyspnea, lightheadedness, distended neck veins and a blood pressure of 86/58 in a man with a known effusion are evidence suggesting cardiac tamponade, a feature associated with higher risk and a potential need for hospitalization. He should go to the emergency department now rather than be managed in the office. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 27"}]};
